the rhetorical device we're going to  discuss today is the slippery slope  argument this is where someone claims  that one small first step can or will  lead to a chain of related events that  result in something consequential  happening usually something undesirable  something bad something we should try to  avoid this strategy can be used properly  but sometimes it's fallacious here's an  example of a slippery slope argument  you've been a regular smoker for some  time but you recently quit you haven't  had a cigarette for three months you go  to a concert and your friend who doesn't  know you've been trying to quit offers  you a cigarette you think to yourself if  I smoke this one cigarette  I'll just want another one later tonight  and then I'll want to buy a pack of  cigarettes tomorrow and then I'll just  become a regular smoker again so you say  to your friend no thanks I'm trying to  quit you've given a slippery slope  argument in response to your friend  you've basically claimed that once one  event occurs other related events will  follow eventually leading to unwanted  consequences the idea is that once you  take that first step down the slope  smoking the first cigarette in our  example then you'll quickly fall down to  the bottom of the slippery slope  becoming someone who smokes regularly  all over again here's what the argument  looks like if a then B if B then C not C  therefore not a in other words premise  if you smoke one cigarette you will  eventually become a regular smoker  premise becoming a regular smoker again  is undesirable premise you shouldn't do  what is undesirable our conclusion being  you shouldn't smoke that first cigarette  to figure out if this is a fallacious  use of a slippery slope argument we have  to evaluate it right the first test  would be looking at premise two are the  claimed effects actually undesirable in  this case yeah right the undisputed  medical consensus is that smoking  regularly is detrimental to your health  let's now test premise one the core of  the slippery slope if you smoke one  cigarette  you will event  truly become a regular smoker well the  science has actually shown that the vast  majority of ex smokers who smoke one  cigarette after quitting can't stop  there and return to their status as  regular smokers so there is at least  good reason to think that this premise  is likely true and third do the costs  outweigh all of the benefits of the  first event are there other relevant  factors that we should consider when it  comes to that first event which might  make the negative consequences worth it  in the end maybe you would enjoy that  one cigarette at the concert so much but  assuming that all of the other premises  are true it puts you at significant risk  for becoming a regular smoker again and  becoming a regular smoker is undesirable  for very good reasons therefore know the  benefits of smoking that first cigarette  probably are not worth the negative  consequences so this particular slippery  slope argument is probably sound but if  any of these premises weren't true there  would be a stopping point in the  slippery slope and then the slippery  slope argument would be fallacious and  unsound take this example premise one if  I join an airline's reward program I  will fly more premise two if I fly more  I will be in a plane crash I don't want  to be in a plane crash the conclusion  therefore is that I shouldn't join an  airline's reward program there are  multiple ways to break this slippery  slope multiple untrue premises premise  one joining an airline rewards program  doesn't necessarily mean that you'll fly  more it just means you'll benefit from  accumulating frequent flyer miles when  you do fly and premise to flying more  often doesn't mean you'll get into a  plane crash commercial flights are one  of the safest modes of transportation  and premise three certainly I don't want  to get into a plane crash but the other  premises don't hold up and we can  evaluate this slippery slope argument as  being unsound and fallacious assessing  the strength of slippery slope arguments  is not always this  black-and-white you've got to take into  consideration the probability that each  of the premises is true in evaluating  the overall soundness of the argument  especially because slippery slope  arguments aren't always intended to be  strictly valid which is to say if a and  B if B then C many people arguing a  slippery slope are merely intending to  say that this is a likely succession of  events so you have to ask yourself how  likely is it that B will follow from a  and C follow from B if it's very likely  then the arguer has a good case with  slippery slopes it boils down to  determining the plausibility and  likelihood of the premises if you're  interested in philosophy and critical  thinking you will love brilliant which  has pretty much become my go-to for  learning things online check out  brilliance new immersive lessons on  logic problem-solving multi level  thinking identifying false information  and identifying fallacies whether you  already have a grasp on any of these  subjects or you're looking for a fun and  engaging way to become acquainted with  these topics for the first time  brilliant really is the place to go  where you can actually learn hands-on by  solving problems and puzzles designed by  expert educators and unlike other  learning sites when I'm using brilliant  I'm actually learning by working  hands-on with real-world scenarios and  problems sign up for free by going to  brilliant org slash critical thinking  I've put a link in the description under  this video and the first 200 people who  visit will get 20% off an annual Premium  Membership visit brilliant org slash  critical thinking
 

